{
  "abstract": "Consider a graph partitioned into independent pairs in which each interface between distinct pairs is empty, a perfect matching, or contains all four possible edges. We prove that a graph without a nine-clique admits nine colors through twelve pairs whenever the four-edge interfaces have disjoint endpoints among the labels. With at most one such interface, thirteen pairs suffice without an edge bound. At fourteen pairs the result holds under the biplanar bound of 156 edges. Thus any ten-chromatic biplanar graph in the one-interface class requires at least fifteen pairs. We prove a sharp clique-preserving completion lemma for disjoint four-edge interfaces. At twelve pairs the cycle space of a complete graph minus a matching reduces the problem to 54 placements of the exceptional edges. At fourteen pairs, degree-list coloring forces sign balance on the low-degree labels. Further cycle equations come from induced blocks whose exterior needs at most seven colors. Of 289,621 marked placements, these reductions leave 372, mapped by explicit permutations to 66 final formulas. Switchable nine-color words resolve the remaining sign choices. The support census, permutations and binary refutations are reproducible. The conditions on full interfaces leave the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem open.",
